    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Corbin Carroll** leading the charge, boasting an elite 85.94 score against Lowder. The combination of a highly favorable 5.25 xERA matchup and **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s dynamic platoon advantage makes him our primary anchor. Similarly, **Vinnie Pasquantino** (71.83) and **Gabriel Moreno** (71.17) provide high-floor upside against suspect arms; **Vinnie Pasquantino** specifically benefits from a 14% boost due to favorable platoon alignment, while **Gabriel Moreno** capitalizes on the same Lowder matchup that elevates the entire top of our order. Despite the daunting presence of Gausman, **Jose Caballero** remains a roster staple; while his score of 46.99 is dampened by a negative platoon shift, his ability to exploit Gausman\u2019s slow delivery keeps him in the mix for those critical, scarce stolen bases.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today\u2019s bench involved **Austin Riley**, who narrowly missed the cut with a 44.90 rating. While **Austin Riley** offers pedigree, the combination of Manaea\u2019s 3.43 xERA and a poor park factor pushed him below our internal efficiency threshold compared to the high-upside deployments of **James Wood** (65.46) and **Max Muncy** (59.63). **James Wood** is particularly compelling today, as his elite .403 xwOBA and top-of-order protection against Castillo keep his production ceiling high despite a neutral platoon. We are closely monitoring the Nola start against **Brice Turang**; while **Brice Turang** is starting, his score of 54.44 is tempered by Nola\u2019s ability to neutralize base-stealers, underscoring our commitment to strict efficiency caps. We remain wary of **Seiya Suzuki** (43.23), whose score is suppressed by a negative park factor and a tough matchup against McDonald, placing him on a short leash to preserve our game caps if the early innings signal a stagnant outing.",
